Fix cross-platform CI: use-after-free test, MSVC narrowing, version system

The second CI run left Linux and clang-format green but macOS and Windows red.
Two genuine portability bugs, plus a first-class version system.

macOS: test_components.cpp read detach_count from a LifecycleProbe after
remove_component() had destroyed it. That is a use-after-free that happened to
read 1 on Linux (the freed byte was untouched) and 0 on macOS (the allocator
scribbled it). The probe now increments counters owned by the test frame, which
outlive the component, so the assertion observes a live object.

Windows: sizeof(u32) is a size_t, and passing it to OpenGLBuffer's u32
element_size parameter is a narrowing conversion that MSVC reports as C4267.
GLFW also failed to build again: FESettings.cmake still applied /WX with
add_compile_options(), which is directory-scoped and leaks into FetchContent
subdirectories. Warnings-as-errors now lives on the fe_warnings interface
target, so /WX reaches our targets only.

Version system: the engine version was only a project() string, invisible to
C++ and untested. cmake/FEVersion.cmake now generates fe/version/Version.h from
that single declaration, exposing fe::version::kEngineVersion (a SemVersion),
kEngineVersionString, and the FE_VERSION_* macros. Engine::initialize logs the
version on startup, and a test asserts the string matches the numeric
components so it cannot drift. The scene file-format version stays separate,
since it evolves on its own schedule.

Verified on Linux/GCC 14 with -DFE_WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS=ON: clean build, 62 test
cases / 2223 assertions pass, clang-format clean.
